last-W tokens in attention, rest recomputed. Retained for context.\nL2b — Attention-sink + sliding window (StreamingLLM-style: K initial sink tokens preserved + sliding window over the recent W). A realistic modern long-context baseline; memory-at-all but no dynamic retrieval.\nL2c (optional, Tier-3) — Chunked precomputed-summary / landmark memory: off-line computed summary vectors indexed by chunk, no query-conditioned retrieval on the live stream. Strong static-memory competitor; isolates whether dynamic query conditioning matters.\nL3 — InfLLM-style dynamic lookup: cached memory units + per-step top-k retrieval into the live window. Reference behavior.\nL4 — L3 with retrieval-frequency sweep: every 1 / 4 / 16 / 64 generated tokens. Isolates cost–quality curve.\nL5 — L3 with retrieval disabled at depth ≥ D (off in middle, on at extremes). Diagnostic: where on the depth axis dynamic retrieval matters.\n\nEffect on interpretation, with L0a/L0b split and L2b added:\n- L3 vs L0a → contribution of any memory mechanism over the base model.\n- L3 vs L0b → contribution of any memory mechanism at matched attention budget.\n- L3 vs L2b → contribution of dynamic retrieval beyond attention-sinks + sliding window — the clean isolation the researcher asked for.\n- L2b vs L2 → contribution of memory-at-all (attention-sinks are a form of memory, not window recollect).\n- L2c vs L2b → contribution of static structured memory over sliding/sink.\n- L3 vs L2c → contribution of dynamic query conditioning over precomputed chunked summary.\n\nThe 2x2 core is preserved but the core baseline on L is now unambiguously L2b (memory-at-all, no dynamic retrieval) rather than L0; L0a/L0b remain for floor-of-comparison interpretation. The researcher-stated core cells (L0 x U_B, L3 x U_T, L3 x U_B, L2 x U_B) are kept as originally stated, with L0 = L0b in the (L0 x U_B) core cell and the new cleaner isolation contrast L3 vs L2b added without expanding the compute budget.\n\n### Axis 2 — Unit representation U (what is embedded / cached / retrieved)\n\nUnchanged: U_T (token), U_B (block), U_S (sentence), U_G (block + cross-attention gate). Similarity (cosine / dot-product / learned gate) folded into U_B / U_G as a sub-cell.\n\nOptional unit-boundary sub-cell under U_B (only if Tier-1 budget allows — same wall-clock, no new axis):\n- U_B.fixed: contiguous fixed-token blocks (the default; e.g., 16 / 64 / 256 token window).\n- U_B.sent: sentence-segmented blocks, capped at the same matched block size (otherwise paired by mean-pooled block size so retrieval cost is identical).\n- U_B.sem: semantic-segmented blocks (e.g., embedding-breakpoint segmentation, off-line), same size cap.\n\nThis isolates whether the unit boundary matters once the unit size is fixed — i.e., shares the rest of U_B's setup so the contrast is purely boundary-conditioned at matched FLOPs and matched query-pooling.\n\n### What is measured (unchanged)\n\nTasks: passkey retrieval, needle-in-a-haystack (depths x distractors x needle counts), multi-hop QA (HotpotQA / 2WikiMultihopQA long variants), long-context summarization (GovReport / SQuALITY with ROUGE-L + LLM-judge faithfulness secondary).\n\nDiagnostics: retrieval precision@K, recall@K per step; passkey/NIAH hit rate by depth bin (0–25%, 25–50%, 50–75%, 75–100%); open-book perplexity on PG-19 / ArXiv-math / CodeParrot with fixed memory budget.\n\nSystem: end-to-end ms/token (mean, P95), KV-cache footprint (GB), retrieval FLOP share.\n\n### Controls (unchanged and now tightened by L0 split)\n\n- Same frozen base LM (>=128k native window — researcher to confirm exact choice).\n- Identical context length within each length-sweep point (4k / 16k / 64k / 128k; 128k may be downscoped — see Feasibility).\n- Identical prompts, decoding settings, seeds for any stochastic component.\n- L0b uses exactly the same working budget as the other cells — this is the point of the split, so the no-memory baseline now lives at the same compute envelope as memory-augmented cells.\n- L2b holds sink count and window size fixed across cells that vary U, and vice versa, so attention-sink memory is not silently growing with U.\n- Matched retrieval budget per cell (top-K tuned so total retrieval FLOPs within 10% across cells); non-dynamic cells receive an equivalent wall-clock step.\n- Matched training data — inference-time cells are training-free; U_G gate (if learned) uses a fixed corpus slice, fixed seed, fixed epochs, held out of evaluation.\n- Identical KV representation; RoPE scaling held constant.\n\n### Analysis plan (unchanged)\n\n- Primary pre-registered contrast: L3xU_B − L2bxU_B at each length, paired bootstrap over (tasks x contexts x seeds), Bonferroni–Holm across metric set, mean ± 95% CI + Cohen's d. This is the contrast that isolates the contribution of dynamic retrieval from memory in general.\n- Secondary contrasts reported alongside: L3xU_B − L0bxU_B (any memory), L3xU_B − L0axU_B (memory+long-window), L2bxU_B − L2xU_B (memory-at-all), L2cxU_B − L2bxU_B (static structured memory), L3xU_B − L2cxU_B (dynamic query conditioning).\n- The 2x2 core reported as specified by the researcher: (L0 x U_B), (L3 x U_T), (L3 x U_B), (L2 x U_B); with L0 = L0b in the (L0 x U_B) cell.\n- Length sweep heatmap: accuracy x task x length x condition; report saturated and non-saturated lengths separately.\n- At least 3 seeds per cell; paired bootstrap over (tasks x contexts x seeds); pre-registration of primary contrast before opening data.\n\n### Feasibility / tiers (unchanged; refinements fit within existing tiers)\n\n- Tier-1 (minimum confident answer): core 2x2 with L0 split into L0a/L0b, plus L2b as a core-baseline extension cell, plus the primary pre-registered L3 vs L2b contrast. Length = 16k only. 4 tasks x 3 seeds. The L0b-vs-L0a sub-contrast is a free sink-cost diagnostic (no extra compute).\n- Tier-2: add L4 retrieval-frequency sweep; add length sweep (4k / 16k / 64k / 128k, with 128k downscoped if VRAM-bound).\n- Tier-