{"id": "4003fd24-d584-4e88-87bb-9a03e9fe4a1c", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T03:29:17.077090", "source": "user", "parent_id": "70ec0140-987e-494b-9658-2ba8ff02ce6a", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. Produce the final experiment design deliverable per the submission contract: a single coherent document with rationale, controls, analysis plan and feasibility, organized as follows. (1) Research question and the two causal factors being isolated (dynamic memory lookup; unit representation). (2) Design overview: 2x2 core factorial (L0 x U_B with L0 = L0b, L3 x U_T, L3 x U_B, L2 x U_B) on a single frozen base long-context LM, plus the L-axis extension cells (L0a, L1, L2b, optional L2c) and U-axis sub-cells (U_B block-size sweep; U_B unit-boundary U_B.fixed / U_B.sent / U_B.sem at matched block size; similarity-metric sub-cell). State explicitly how L3 vs L2b isolates dynamic retrieval, L2b vs L2 isolates memory-at-all, L3 vs L2c isolates dynamic query conditioning, and L0a vs L0b disambiguates the no-memory floor. (3) Variables and controls: frozen base model, identical context length per length-sweep point (4k / 16k / 64k / 128k), matched working attention budget, matched retrieval/wall-clock budget (top-K tuned so retrieval FLOPs within 10%, or paired wall-clock steps), matched KV representation and RoPE scaling, matched prompts and decoding, fixed seeds; explicitly enumerate what is held constant and what varies. (4) Task battery and metrics: passkey / NIAH across depth bins and needle counts, multi-hop QA (HotpotQA, 2WikiMultihopQA long variants), long summarization (GovReport, SQuALITY with ROUGE-L + LLM-judge faithfulness secondary); plus per-step diagnostics (retrieval precision@K, recall@K, passkey/NIAH hit rate by depth bin) and system metrics (ms/token mean and P95, KV footprint GB, retrieval FLOP share); plus open-book perplexity on PG-19 / ArXiv-math / CodeParrot at fixed memory budget. (5) Analysis plan: pre-registered primary contrast L3 x U_B - L2b x U_B; secondary contrasts (L3 - L0a, L3 - L0b, L2b - L2, L2c - L2b, L3 - L2c) and the U-axis decomposition (block-size sweep, similarity-metric sub-cell, unit-boundary sub-cell); length-sweep heatmap accuracy x task x length x condition with saturated vs non-saturated lengths separated; >=3 seeds; paired bootstrap over (tasks x contexts x seeds); Bonferroni-Holm correction across the metric set; mean +/- 95% CI and Cohen's d for each primary contrast; pre-registration before opening data; explicitly note the inflation budget implications. (6) Feasibility tiers: Tier-1 minimum confident answer (core 2x2 with L0a/L0b, L2b as core-baseline extension cell, primary L3 vs L2b contrast, length = 16k, 4 tasks x 3 seeds), Tier-2 (length sweep + L4 retrieval-frequency sweep), Tier-3 (L2c chunked-summary, U_B unit-boundary sub-cell, U_G learned gate controlled training extension); for each tier give approximate GPU-hour order and on which axis compute is bound; record that this deliverable is design-only and that execution is not required per track.md. (7) Threats to validity and limitations: include assumptions A1-A6 and uncertainties U1-U7 verbatim, and add (i) whether InfLLM's published retrieval rule is faithfully replicated without source, (ii) the risk that the base model already has strong long-context behavior that compresses the headroom for memory effects, (iii) confounds between similarity metric and pooling choice for U_B. (8) Clarity notes: explicitly state A1 as a load-bearing assumption that the design mitigates by anchoring the primary contrast on the L3 vs L2b direction (dynamic retrieval vs attention-sink+sliding window) which is robust to small spec drift. Do not invent execution results, runtime measurements, or expected outcomes. The deliverable should be self-contained and fit the submission contract; cite no fabricated performance numbers.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 21}
{"id": "4e334866-23f5-4ec3-9e6c-007f40f7820a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T03:30:52.608421", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "4003fd24-d584-4e88-87bb-9a03e9fe4a1c", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Criticality of Dynamic Memory Lookup and Unit Representation in InfLLM\n\nDesign-only deliverable per track.md. No experiments were executed; no runtime or accuracy figures are reported. The document below is the submission.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question and causal factors\n\nPublic question: *How critical are dynamic context memory lookup and the choice of unit representations to InfLLM's performance across long-context tasks?*\n\nThe question separates into two causal factors that the design isolates independently and in combination:\n\n- **F1 — Dynamic memory lookup.** Whether *query-conditioned retrieval at inference time* (as opposed to fixed, sliding, or static memory mechanisms) is what drives InfLLM's long-context gains.\n- **F2 — Unit representation.** Whether the *granularity and boundary* at which past context is stored, indexed, and matched (token vs contiguous block vs sentence/semantic segment, with various pooling and similarity choices) materially changes performance when F1 is held fixed.\n\nThe causal decomposition below is the core of the design.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design overview\n\n### 2.1 Setup\n\nA single **frozen base long-context LM** (≥128k native window; exact choice to be confirmed) acts as the shared substrate. **No fine-tuning** of the base model. Per cell, only the inference-time KV-cache policy (L-axis), the unit policy (U-axis), and (optionally) the similarity metric vary; everything else is held constant.\n\nThis isolates F1 because retrieval is the only moving part across L cells, and isolates F2 because the L cell is the only moving part across U cells.\n\n### 2.2 Primary 2×2 core factorial\n\n| | **U_T (token)** | **U_B (block)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L0** *(= L0b; no memory, attention truncated to working budget)* | — | (L0 × U_B) |\n| **L2** *(plain sliding window, no dynamic retrieval)* | — | (L2 × U_B) |\n| **L3** *(InfLLM dynamic lookup + retrieval)* | (L3 × U_T) | (L3 × U_B) ← reference cell |\n\nThe four core cells are (L0 × U_B), (L3 × U_T), (L3 × U_B), (L2 × U_B). L0 here means L0b (no memory, attention truncated to working budget).\n\n### 2.3 L-axis extension cells\n\n- **L0a** — native base-model attention up to its native window, no memory. \"What the base model can already do\" — clean floor.\n- **L0b** — same base model, attention truncated to the working budget used by every other cell, no memory. Aligns the no-memory cell with the compute envelope of memory-augmented cells.\n- **L1** — fixed/recurrent cache: first-N tokens always retained, rest evicted. Fixed-memory ablation.\n- **L2** — plain sliding window: last-W tokens in attention, rest recomputed.\n- **L2b** — attention-sink + sliding window (StreamingLLM-style): a small number of initial sink KVs preserved + sliding window over the recent W. Realistic modern long-context baseline; \"memory-at-all\" but no dynamic retrieval on the live stream.\n- **L2c** *(optional, Tier-3)* — chunked precomputed-summary / landmark memory: off-line computed summary vectors indexed by chunk, no query-conditioned retrieval on the live stream.\n- **L3** — InfLLM-style dynamic lookup: cached memory units + per-step top-k retrieval into the live window.\n- **L4** — L3 with retrieval-frequency sweep: every 1 / 4 / 16 / 64 generated tokens.\n- **L5** — L3 with retrieval disabled at depth ≥ D (off in middle, on at extremes). Diagnostic.\n\n### 2.4 Causal contrast map\n\n- **L3 × U_B − L0a × U_B** → *any memory mechanism* over the base model (memory + long window).\n- **L3 × U_B − L0b × U_B** → *any memory mechanism* at matched attention budget.\n- **L2b × U_B − L2 × U_B** → *memory-at-all* (attention-sinks count as memory).\n- **L3 × U_B − L2b × U_B** → **dynamic retrieval** beyond attention-sink + sliding window. **Pre-registered primary contrast.** Cleanest direct test of F1.\n- **L2c × U_B − L2b × U_B** → static structured memory over sliding/sink.\n- **L3 × U_B − L2c × U_B** → *dynamic query conditioning* over precomputed chunked summary.\n- **L0a × U_B − L0b × U_B** → disambiguates the *no-memory floor*: distinguishes the base model's native long-context behavior from its budgeted behavior.\n\n### 2.5 U-axis sub-cells\n\nWithin U_B:\n- **Block-size sweep** (Tier-2): 16 / 64 / 256 tokens; reference cell at 16.\n- **Unit-boundary sub-cell** *(Tier-3, optional)* at matched mean block size and matched retrieval cost:\n  - U_B.fixed: contiguous fixed-token blocks.\n  - U_B.sent: sentence-segmented blocks, capped at matched size.\n  - U_B.sem: semantic-segmented blocks (embedding-breakpoint, off-line), capped at matched size.\n- **Similarity-metric sub-cell**: cosine / dot-product / learned gate, reported as a sub-row of U_B / U_G.\n\nU_T is reported once. U_S is reported as a U-axis extension (Tier-3). U_G (block + cross-attention gate) is reported with analytic formulation (default) and learned fallback (Tier-3), with training data, seed, and epoch count fixed across variants.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Variables and controls\n\n### 3.1 What varies (axes of the design)\n\n- L cell: L0a, L0b, L1, L2, L2b, L2c, L3, L4, L5.\n- U cell: U_T, U_B (fixed / sent / sem), U_S, U_G (analytic / learned).\n- Block size under U_B: 16 / 64 / 256.\n- Similarity metric under U_B: cosine / dot-product / gate.\n- Retrieval frequency under L4: every 1 / 4 / 16 / 64 generated tokens.\n- Length: 4k / 16k / 64k / 128k (16k is Tier-1; 4k/64k/128k are Tier-2 / Tier-3).\n- Seed: ≥3 per cell.\n\n### 3.2 What is held constant (controls)\n\n- **Base model.** A single frozen open long-context LM with ≥128k native window (final choice to be confirmed). All cells share weights, tokenizer, attention operator, and RoPE scaling.\n- **Working attention budget.** Identical for L0b, L1, L2, L2b, L3, L4, L5, and (Tier-3) L2c. This is the major tightening: the no-memory baseline now lives at the same compute envelope as memory-augmented cells.\n- **Matched retrieval / wall-clock budget.**\n  - For dynamic-lookup cells (L3, L4, L5), top-K is selected so total retrieval FLOPs differ by ≤10% across U cells at a given length; the rest of the inference step uses the same base-model forward graph.\n  - For non-dynamic cells (L0a, L0b, L1, L2, L2b), the wall-clock step uses the same matched envelope. L2b's sink count and window size are fixed across cells that vary U.\n  - For L2c, off-line summary cost is not on the live critical path but is fixed and reported.\n  - The U_B unit-boundary sub-cell pairs cells by mean retrieval cost: varying boundary only at matched mean block size; varying size only inside block.\n- **KV representation.** Identical across all cells; no positional encoding beyond the base model's RoPE; RoPE scaling held constant.\n- **Prompts, decoding, seeds.** Identical prompts; deterministic decoding for tasks where generation quality is graded (T = 0 unless the researcher prefers sampling with fixed seed); ≥3 seeds per cell for any stochastic component (block-sampling order, gate dropout).\n- **Training data for the gate (U_G only).** Fixed corpus slice, fixed epochs, fixed seed for both analytic and learned variants; held out of evaluation.\n- **Pre-registration of contrasts and metric set** before opening any data.\n\n### 3.3 Out of scope\n\n- Fine-tuning the base model.\n- Architectural changes to attention (no modification of the base model's attention operator).\n- Multi-model ensembling / mixture-of-experts.\n- External retrieval-augmented generation against external corpora — the F1 question concerns *internal* memory lookup.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Task battery and metrics\n\n### 4.1 Capability tasks\n\n- **Passkey retrieval.** RULER-style single fact inserted into long context. The most sensitive probe for memory lookup.\n- **Needle-in-a-Haystack (NIAH).** Multiple depths × multiple distractors × multiple needle counts. Reported as hit rate by depth bin (0–25%, 25–50%, 50–75%, 75–100% of context).\n- **Multi-hop QA.** Long-context variants of HotpotQA and 2WikiMultihopQA. Reasoning across separated facts stresses F1's *coverage* contribution.\n- **Long-context summarization.** GovReport (long government-report summarization) and SQuALITY (long-document QA-style summarization). Primary metric ROUGE-L; secondary LLM-judge faithfulness.\n\n### 4.2 Per-step diagnostic metrics\n\n- **Retrieval precision@K, recall@K** at each lookup step (meaningful when F1 is active, i.e., L ∈ {L3, L4, L5}, or F2 defines retrievable units, i.e., U ∈ {U_B, U_S, U_G}).\n- **Passkey / NIAH hit rate by depth bin**, separating an attention-budget cliff from a coverage cliff.\n\n### 4.3 System metrics\n\n- End-to-end **ms/token** (mean and P95).\n- **KV-cache footprint (GB)** at each length.\n- **Retrieval FLOP share** — fraction of step FLOPs attributable to the lookup mechanism, reported per L cell per U cell.\n\n### 4.4 Open-book perplexity\n\nOpen-book perplexity on held-out long-context corpora (PG-19, ArXiv-math, CodeParrot) with the memory budget fixed across cells. Captures language-modeling quality under constrained memory and is computed at every length-sweep point.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Analysis plan\n\n### 5.1 Pre-registration\n\nBefore opening any data, register in writing:\n- the **primary contrast** and its direction;\n- the **metric set** that contributes to the primary contrast;\n- the **Tier-1 / Tier-2 / Tier-3** structure;\n- the **multiple-comparison correction** procedure;\n- the **seed count**;\n- the **stopping rules**.\n\nAny deviation after data is opened is reported as exploratory, not confirmatory.\n\n### 5.2 Pre-registered primary contrast\n\n**L3 × U_B − L2b × U_B** at each length, on the pre-registered metric set. This is the contrast that isolates *dynamic retrieval* (F1) cleanly from memory-in-general, by anchoring on attention-sink + sliding window as the strongest realistic no-dynamic-retrieval competitor.\n\n### 5.3 Secondary contrasts\n\n- L3 × U_B − L0a × U_B: any memory over the base model.\n- L3 × U_B − L0b × U_B: any memory at matched attention budget.\n- L2b × U_B − L2 × U_B: memory-at-all (sinks count as memory).\n- L2c × U_B − L2b × U_B: static structured memory over sliding/sink.\n- L3 × U_B − L2c × U_B: dynamic query conditioning over precomputed chunked summary.\n- L0a × U_B − L0b × U_B: no-memory floor disambiguation.\n\n### 5.4 U-axis decomposition (isolating F2)\n\n- Block-size sweep under U_B (16 / 64 / 256): isolates the size dimension of F2.\n- Similarity-metric sub-cell (cosine / dot-product / gate) under U_B: isolates the matching dimension of F2.\n- Unit-boundary sub-cell (U_B.fixed / U_B.sent / U_B.sem) at matched mean block size: isolates the boundary dimension of F2.\n- U_T vs U_B contrast under L3: isolates the granularity dimension of F2.\n\n### 5.5 Length sweep\n\nLengths 4k / 16k / 64k / 128k. Reported as a heatmap: **accuracy × task × length × condition**, with saturated and non-saturated lengths reported separately. 128k may be downscoped if VRAM-bound (see Feasibility).\n\n### 5.6 Statistical reporting\n\n- **Seeds.** ≥3 per cell.\n- **Paired bootstrap.** Differential (variant − baseline) over the joint randomness surface (tasks × contexts × seeds), with bootstrap resampling preserving pair structure; BCa or percentile intervals.\n- **Multiple-comparison correction.** Bonferroni–Holm across the metric set within each contrast family; for the secondary contrasts, family-wise correction across the contrast family.\n- **Effect size.** Mean ± 95% CI; Cohen's d for each primary contrast.\n- **Inflation budget.** The metric set is the dominant multiplier. With M metrics and C contrast candidates per cell × length × task cell, the family-wise correction controls FWER at α within each priority band; outside that band (decomposition, post-hoc), results are exploratory. The pre-registered priority order is:\n  1. Primary contrast (L3 − L2b).\n  2. Secondary contrasts (L3 − L0a, L3 − L0b, L2b − L2, L2c − L2b, L3 − L2c, L0a − L0b).\n  3. U-axis decomposition and length-sweep extension.\n\nThis bounds worst-case inflation while admitting post-hoc decomposition. The correction is applied within bands 1–2; band 3 is reported with effect size and CI but explicitly labelled exploratory.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Feasibility tiers\n\nThis deliverable is **design-only**. No experiment is executed; no runtime or accuracy figures are reported. The orders below describe what execution *would* entail, not what it has produced.\n\n| Tier | Cells | Lengths | Tasks × seeds | Approx GPU-hour order | Compute binding |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Tier-1 (minimum confident answer)** | Core 2×2 + L0a + L2b; primary contrast L3×U_B vs L2b×U_B | 16k only | 4 × 3 | Low tens of GPU-hours on a single A100/H100; long-context forward pass dominates at 16k | Single forward pass per cell |\n| **Tier-2** | Adds L4 retrieval-frequency sweep; adds length sweep | 4k / 16k / 64k / 128k (128k downscoped if VRAM-bound) | 4 × 3 | Several × Tier-1 | Wall-clock × retrieval-frequency at 128k |\n| **Tier-3** | Adds L2c; U_B unit-boundary sub-cell; U_G learned gate | All of the above | 4 × 3 | Several × again, now U_G training dominates | U_G controlled training + 128k evaluation |\n\nWithin Tier-3, the U_B unit-boundary sub-cell and the U_G learned-gate extension are deliberately contained: gating adds parameters but the training data, seed, and epochs are fixed across cells that include it.\n\n**Offline / analytically tractable decomposition.** The U-axis (unit / pooling / similarity) and the L4 retrieval-frequency sweep are runnable on a fixed short context (4k–8k) using only retrieval-quality metrics (precision@K, recall@K) and passkey — no real long-context generation. Early signal at near-zero cost precedes the bulk Tier-1 evaluation.\n\nThis deliverable is design-only; no runtime measurements are recorded.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Threats to validity and limitations\n\n### 7.1 Assumptions (verbatim)\n\n- **A1** InfLLM = training-free KV-cache architecture with cached memory units + per-step dynamic top-k retrieval. Pending reconfirmation against the paper (U1).\n- **A2** A single frozen open long-context LM (≥128k native window). Researcher to confirm.\n- **A3** Inference-time policy holds for L0 through L3; U_G preferred analytic; if learned, fixed corpus / seed / epochs.\n- **A4** Memory = cached past K/V units indexed by hidden-state query; not parametric memory injection.\n- **A5** Default task battery (passkey / NIAH / multi-hop QA / long summarization) is interchangeable if the researcher prefers alternatives.\n- **A6** Attention-sink baseline L2b uses a small fixed number of initial-token sink KVs (StreamingLLM convention); sink count tuned once and frozen.\n\n### 7.2 Uncertainties (verbatim)\n\n- **U1** Reference fidelity of L3×U_B to the published InfLLM specification; reduced severity now that L2b makes the contrast robust to spec drift on the dynamic-retrieval side.\n- **U2** Base model identity affects absolute performance, not the comparative interpretation.\n- **U3** 128k feasibility depends on VRAM; may move to Tier-3.\n- **U4** Whether to include learned U_G gate.\n- **U5** Statistical unit for paired bootstrap (items vs seeds).\n- **U6** Whether retrieval-frequency (L4) is core or extension — currently Tier-2.\n- **U7** Sink count for L2b: default is tune-once + freeze; researcher may prefer a sink-count sweep in Tier-3.\n\n### 7.3 Additional threats to validity\n\n- **V1 — Reference fidelity without source materials.** This deliverable was produced without the InfLLM paper or code in the workspace. The L3×U_B reference cell is reconstructed from public description (see A1). Specific retention / eviction rules, top-k schedule, training-free claim, and how retrieval interacts with the base model's positional encoding cannot be reconfirmed in-repo. Mitigation in §8.\n- **V2 — Compressed headroom.** Modern long-context base models may already capture a substantial portion of the long-context behavior that memory mechanisms add. The dynamic-retrieval signal can therefore be small in absolute terms even when meaningful as a factor. The length sweep (4k / 16k / 64k / 128k) plus depth-bin decomposition lets us see whether the L3 − L2b gap widens or narrows with length; this is the cleanest signal of whether F1 has any headroom to claim. Where headroom is small, the deliverable is the *comparative Δ with bootstrap CIs and effect sizes*, not the absolute number.\n- **V3 — Confounding between similarity metric and pooling choice under U_B.** Under U_B, mean-pooled query + cosine similarity is the default; switching to last-token + dot-product conflates two factors at once. Mitigation: factor the similarity sub-cell out as its own row, and report U_T vs U_B as the primary granularity contrast, with the similarity sub-cell as a secondary decomposition. Effect-size reporting at the similarity sub-cell is exploratory to pre-registration and is not used to overturn the primary contrast.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Clarity note: A1 is load-bearing, the design mitigates A1\n\nA1 (faithful reconstruction of InfLLM from public description without the paper or code in the repo) is the central load-bearing assumption. If A1 were strongly wrong — that is, if the published InfLLM spec uses a substantially different retrieval rule, retention schedule, or KV-cache layout than the reconstruction — the L3×U_B reference cell itself would be misspecified, and any direct claim about InfLLM would inherit that misspecification.\n\nThe design mitigates this in two complementary ways:\n\n1. **The primary contrast is direction-anchored, not absolute.** L3 − L2b asks whether *dynamic retrieval* as a mechanism class outperforms the strongest realistic no-dynamic-retrieval competitor (attention-sink + sliding window). The direction of the contrast survives small spec drift: moderate misspecification of the L3 cell cannot flip a dynamic-retrieval effect into a no-dynamic-retrieval effect, because both cells are instantiated within the design and both reconstructions are anchored in public description. The contrast is robust to A1 misspecification as long as the L3 cell implements *some* dynamic query-conditioned retrieval on cached units — the most stable part of the public description across reasonable reconstructions.\n2. **Pre-registration locks the contrast and the metric set.** Before opening data, we register L3 × U_B − L2b × U_B as primary. If post-hoc we discover a more faithful InfLLM reconstruction, it is reported separately as exploratory rather than allowed to redefine the primary contrast.\n\nCombined, these measures mean that *even if A1 is partially misspecified, the primary conclusion still answers the public question honestly*: it tells us how much dynamic retrieval contributes on top of the strongest realistic non-dynamic memory baseline, with the comparative rather than absolute interpretation. A1's severity is therefore reduced from \"blocks the experiment\" to \"shapes the absolute numbers and the precise L3 cell, but not the comparative direction.\"\n\n---\n\n*End of design-only deliverable. No experiments were executed; no runtime, accuracy, or other empirical numbers are reported.*", "evidence": "Repository inspection (inspect_repository on /workspace plus recursive finds for *infllm*, *README*, *paper*, *track.md`) returned only /workspace/task.json (433 bytes), whose own documents.track.md field is the placeholder \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No InfLLM paper, code, configs, READMEs, notes, scripts, or datasets exist anywhere accessible; network is disabled, no GPU, 24 CPUs, Python 3.12.3 present, no project deps preinstalled. The design is constructed from public-description knowledge of InfLLM (training-free KV-cache memory + dynamic top-k retrieval), with that gap flagged as A1 / U1 and the primary contrast direction-anchored against attention-sink + sliding window (L2b) so that the comparison is robust to small reconstruction drift.", "assumptions": ["A1 InfLLM = training-free KV-cache architecture with cached memory units + per-step dynamic top-k retrieval. Pending reconfirmation against the paper (U1).", "A2 A single frozen open long-context LM (>=128k native window). Researcher to confirm.", "A3 Inference-time policy holds for L0 through L3; U_G preferred analytic; if learned, fixed corpus / seed / epochs.", "A4 Memory = cached past K/V units indexed by hidden-state query; not parametric memory injection.", "A5 Default task battery (passkey / NIAH / multi-hop QA / long summarization) is interchangeable if the researcher prefers alternatives.", "A6 Attention-sink baseline L2b uses a small fixed number of initial-token sink KVs (StreamingLLM convention); sink count tuned once and frozen."], "uncertainties": ["U1 Reference fidelity of L3 x U_B to the published InfLLM specification; reduced severity now that L2b makes the contrast robust to spec drift on the dynamic-retrieval side.", "U2 Base model identity affects absolute performance, not the comparative interpretation.", "U3 128k feasibility depends on VRAM; may move to Tier-3.", "U4 Whether to include learned U_G gate.", "U5 Statistical unit for paired bootstrap (items vs seeds).", "U6 Whether retrieval-frequency (L4) is core or extension - currently Tier-2.", "U7 Sink count for L2b: default is tune-once + freeze; researcher may prefer a sink-count sweep in Tier-3."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-0e85505d-0940-4179-b3b7-824bd6980613", "tool_call": {"id": "call-0e85505d-0940-4179-b3b7-824bd6980613", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final design deliverable (result stage)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design: Criticality of Dynamic Memory Lookup and Unit Representation in InfLLM\\n\\nDesign-only deliverable per track.md. No experiments were executed; no runtime or accuracy figures are reported. The document below is the submission.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Research question and causal factors\\n\\nPublic question: *How critical are dynamic context memory lookup and the choice of unit representations to InfLLM's performance across long-context tasks?*\\n\\nThe question separates into two causal factors that the design isolates independently and in combination:\\n\\n- **F1 \\u2014 Dynamic memory lookup.** Whether *query-conditioned retrieval at inference time* (as opposed to fixed, sliding, or static memory mechanisms) is what drives InfLLM's long-context gains.\\n- **F2 \\u2014 Unit representation.** Whether the *granularity and boundary* at which past context is stored, indexed, and matched (token vs contiguous block vs sentence/semantic segment, with various pooling and similarity choices) materially changes performance when F1 is held fixed.\\n\\nThe causal decomposition below is the core of the design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Design overview\\n\\n### 2.1 Setup\\n\\nA single **frozen base long-context LM** (\\u2265128k native window; exact choice to be confirmed) acts as the shared substrate. **No fine-tuning** of the base model. Per cell, only the inference-time KV-cache policy (L-axis), the unit policy (U-axis), and (optionally) the similarity metric vary; everything else is held constant.\\n\\nThis isolates F1 because retrieval is the only moving part across L cells, and isolates F2 because the L cell is the only moving part across U cells.\\n\\n### 2.2 Primary 2\\u00d72 core factorial\\n\\n| | **U_T (token)** | **U_B (block)** |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **L0** *(= L0b; no memory, attention truncated to working budget)* | \\u2014 | (L0 \\u00d7 U_B) |\\n| **L2** *(plain sliding window, no dynamic retrieval)* | \\u2014 | (L2 \\u00d7 U_B) |\\n| **L3** *(InfLLM dynamic lookup + retrieval)* | (L3 \\u00d7 U_T) | (L3 \\u00d7 U_B) \\u2190 reference cell |\\n\\nThe four core cells are (L0 \\u00d7 U_B), (L3 \\u00d7 U_T), (L3 \\u00d7 U_B), (L2 \\u00d7 U_B). L0 here means L0b (no memory, attention truncated to working budget).\\n\\n### 2.3 L-axis extension cells\\n\\n- **L0a** \\u2014 native base-model attention up to its native window, no memory. \\\"What the base model can already do\\\" \\u2014 clean floor.\\n- **L0b** \\u2014 same base model, attention truncated to the working budget used by every other cell, no memory. Aligns the no-memory cell with the compute envelope of memory-augmented cells.\\n- **L1** \\u2014 fixed/recurrent cache: first-N tokens always retained, rest evicted. Fixed-memory ablation.\\n- **L2** \\u2014 plain sliding window: last-W tokens in attention, rest recomputed.\\n- **L2b** \\u2014 attention-sink + sliding window (StreamingLLM-style): a small number of initial sink KVs preserved + sliding window over the recent W. Realistic modern long-context baseline; \\\"memory-at-all\\\" but no dynamic retrieval on the live stream.\\n- **L2c** *(optional, Tier-3)* \\u2014 chunked precomputed-summary / landmark memory: off-line computed summary vectors indexed by chunk, no query-conditioned retrieval on the live stream.\\n- **L3** \\u2014 InfLLM-style dynamic lookup: cached memory units + per-step top-k retrieval into the live window.\\n- **L4** \\u2014 L3 with retrieval-frequency sweep: every 1 / 4 / 16 / 64 generated tokens.\\n- **L5** \\u2014 L3 with retrieval disabled at depth \\u2265 D (off in middle, on at extremes). Diagnostic.\\n\\n### 2.4 Causal contrast map\\n\\n- **L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L0a \\u00d7 U_B** \\u2192 *any memory mechanism* over the base model (memory + long window).\\n- **L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L0b \\u00d7 U_B** \\u2192 *any memory mechanism* at matched attention budget.\\n- **L2b \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2 \\u00d7 U_B** \\u2192 *memory-at-all* (attention-sinks count as memory).\\n- **L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2b \\u00d7 U_B** \\u2192 **dynamic retrieval** beyond attention-sink + sliding window. **Pre-registered primary contrast.** Cleanest direct test of F1.\\n- **L2c \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2b \\u00d7 U_B** \\u2192 static structured memory over sliding/sink.\\n- **L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2c \\u00d7 U_B** \\u2192 *dynamic query conditioning* over precomputed chunked summary.\\n- **L0a \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L0b \\u00d7 U_B** \\u2192 disambiguates the *no-memory floor*: distinguishes the base model's native long-context behavior from its budgeted behavior.\\n\\n### 2.5 U-axis sub-cells\\n\\nWithin U_B:\\n- **Block-size sweep** (Tier-2): 16 / 64 / 256 tokens; reference cell at 16.\\n- **Unit-boundary sub-cell** *(Tier-3, optional)* at matched mean block size and matched retrieval cost:\\n  - U_B.fixed: contiguous fixed-token blocks.\\n  - U_B.sent: sentence-segmented blocks, capped at matched size.\\n  - U_B.sem: semantic-segmented blocks (embedding-breakpoint, off-line), capped at matched size.\\n- **Similarity-metric sub-cell**: cosine / dot-product / learned gate, reported as a sub-row of U_B / U_G.\\n\\nU_T is reported once. U_S is reported as a U-axis extension (Tier-3). U_G (block + cross-attention gate) is reported with analytic formulation (default) and learned fallback (Tier-3), with training data, seed, and epoch count fixed across variants.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Variables and controls\\n\\n### 3.1 What varies (axes of the design)\\n\\n- L cell: L0a, L0b, L1, L2, L2b, L2c, L3, L4, L5.\\n- U cell: U_T, U_B (fixed / sent / sem), U_S, U_G (analytic / learned).\\n- Block size under U_B: 16 / 64 / 256.\\n- Similarity metric under U_B: cosine / dot-product / gate.\\n- Retrieval frequency under L4: every 1 / 4 / 16 / 64 generated tokens.\\n- Length: 4k / 16k / 64k / 128k (16k is Tier-1; 4k/64k/128k are Tier-2 / Tier-3).\\n- Seed: \\u22653 per cell.\\n\\n### 3.2 What is held constant (controls)\\n\\n- **Base model.** A single frozen open long-context LM with \\u2265128k native window (final choice to be confirmed). All cells share weights, tokenizer, attention operator, and RoPE scaling.\\n- **Working attention budget.** Identical for L0b, L1, L2, L2b, L3, L4, L5, and (Tier-3) L2c. This is the major tightening: the no-memory baseline now lives at the same compute envelope as memory-augmented cells.\\n- **Matched retrieval / wall-clock budget.**\\n  - For dynamic-lookup cells (L3, L4, L5), top-K is selected so total retrieval FLOPs differ by \\u226410% across U cells at a given length; the rest of the inference step uses the same base-model forward graph.\\n  - For non-dynamic cells (L0a, L0b, L1, L2, L2b), the wall-clock step uses the same matched envelope. L2b's sink count and window size are fixed across cells that vary U.\\n  - For L2c, off-line summary cost is not on the live critical path but is fixed and reported.\\n  - The U_B unit-boundary sub-cell pairs cells by mean retrieval cost: varying boundary only at matched mean block size; varying size only inside block.\\n- **KV representation.** Identical across all cells; no positional encoding beyond the base model's RoPE; RoPE scaling held constant.\\n- **Prompts, decoding, seeds.** Identical prompts; deterministic decoding for tasks where generation quality is graded (T = 0 unless the researcher prefers sampling with fixed seed); \\u22653 seeds per cell for any stochastic component (block-sampling order, gate dropout).\\n- **Training data for the gate (U_G only).** Fixed corpus slice, fixed epochs, fixed seed for both analytic and learned variants; held out of evaluation.\\n- **Pre-registration of contrasts and metric set** before opening any data.\\n\\n### 3.3 Out of scope\\n\\n- Fine-tuning the base model.\\n- Architectural changes to attention (no modification of the base model's attention operator).\\n- Multi-model ensembling / mixture-of-experts.\\n- External retrieval-augmented generation against external corpora \\u2014 the F1 question concerns *internal* memory lookup.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Task battery and metrics\\n\\n### 4.1 Capability tasks\\n\\n- **Passkey retrieval.** RULER-style single fact inserted into long context. The most sensitive probe for memory lookup.\\n- **Needle-in-a-Haystack (NIAH).** Multiple depths \\u00d7 multiple distractors \\u00d7 multiple needle counts. Reported as hit rate by depth bin (0\\u201325%, 25\\u201350%, 50\\u201375%, 75\\u2013100% of context).\\n- **Multi-hop QA.** Long-context variants of HotpotQA and 2WikiMultihopQA. Reasoning across separated facts stresses F1's *coverage* contribution.\\n- **Long-context summarization.** GovReport (long government-report summarization) and SQuALITY (long-document QA-style summarization). Primary metric ROUGE-L; secondary LLM-judge faithfulness.\\n\\n### 4.2 Per-step diagnostic metrics\\n\\n- **Retrieval precision@K, recall@K** at each lookup step (meaningful when F1 is active, i.e., L \\u2208 {L3, L4, L5}, or F2 defines retrievable units, i.e., U \\u2208 {U_B, U_S, U_G}).\\n- **Passkey / NIAH hit rate by depth bin**, separating an attention-budget cliff from a coverage cliff.\\n\\n### 4.3 System metrics\\n\\n- End-to-end **ms/token** (mean and P95).\\n- **KV-cache footprint (GB)** at each length.\\n- **Retrieval FLOP share** \\u2014 fraction of step FLOPs attributable to the lookup mechanism, reported per L cell per U cell.\\n\\n### 4.4 Open-book perplexity\\n\\nOpen-book perplexity on held-out long-context corpora (PG-19, ArXiv-math, CodeParrot) with the memory budget fixed across cells. Captures language-modeling quality under constrained memory and is computed at every length-sweep point.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Analysis plan\\n\\n### 5.1 Pre-registration\\n\\nBefore opening any data, register in writing:\\n- the **primary contrast** and its direction;\\n- the **metric set** that contributes to the primary contrast;\\n- the **Tier-1 / Tier-2 / Tier-3** structure;\\n- the **multiple-comparison correction** procedure;\\n- the **seed count**;\\n- the **stopping rules**.\\n\\nAny deviation after data is opened is reported as exploratory, not confirmatory.\\n\\n### 5.2 Pre-registered primary contrast\\n\\n**L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2b \\u00d7 U_B** at each length, on the pre-registered metric set. This is the contrast that isolates *dynamic retrieval* (F1) cleanly from memory-in-general, by anchoring on attention-sink + sliding window as the strongest realistic no-dynamic-retrieval competitor.\\n\\n### 5.3 Secondary contrasts\\n\\n- L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L0a \\u00d7 U_B: any memory over the base model.\\n- L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L0b \\u00d7 U_B: any memory at matched attention budget.\\n- L2b \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2 \\u00d7 U_B: memory-at-all (sinks count as memory).\\n- L2c \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2b \\u00d7 U_B: static structured memory over sliding/sink.\\n- L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2c \\u00d7 U_B: dynamic query conditioning over precomputed chunked summary.\\n- L0a \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L0b \\u00d7 U_B: no-memory floor disambiguation.\\n\\n### 5.4 U-axis decomposition (isolating F2)\\n\\n- Block-size sweep under U_B (16 / 64 / 256): isolates the size dimension of F2.\\n- Similarity-metric sub-cell (cosine / dot-product / gate) under U_B: isolates the matching dimension of F2.\\n- Unit-boundary sub-cell (U_B.fixed / U_B.sent / U_B.sem) at matched mean block size: isolates the boundary dimension of F2.\\n- U_T vs U_B contrast under L3: isolates the granularity dimension of F2.\\n\\n### 5.5 Length sweep\\n\\nLengths 4k / 16k / 64k / 128k. Reported as a heatmap: **accuracy \\u00d7 task \\u00d7 length \\u00d7 condition**, with saturated and non-saturated lengths reported separately. 128k may be downscoped if VRAM-bound (see Feasibility).\\n\\n### 5.6 Statistical reporting\\n\\n- **Seeds.** \\u22653 per cell.\\n- **Paired bootstrap.** Differential (variant \\u2212 baseline) over the joint randomness surface (tasks \\u00d7 contexts \\u00d7 seeds), with bootstrap resampling preserving pair structure; BCa or percentile intervals.\\n- **Multiple-comparison correction.** Bonferroni\\u2013Holm across the metric set within each contrast family; for the secondary contrasts, family-wise correction across the contrast family.\\n- **Effect size.** Mean \\u00b1 95% CI; Cohen's d for each primary contrast.\\n- **Inflation budget.** The metric set is the dominant multiplier. With M metrics and C contrast candidates per cell \\u00d7 length \\u00d7 task cell, the family-wise correction controls FWER at \\u03b1 within each priority band; outside that band (decomposition, post-hoc), results are exploratory. The pre-registered priority order is:\\n  1. Primary contrast (L3 \\u2212 L2b).\\n  2. Secondary contrasts (L3 \\u2212 L0a, L3 \\u2212 L0b, L2b \\u2212 L2, L2c \\u2212 L2b, L3 \\u2212 L2c, L0a \\u2212 L0b).\\n  3. U-axis decomposition and length-sweep extension.\\n\\nThis bounds worst-case inflation while admitting post-hoc decomposition. The correction is applied within bands 1\\u20132; band 3 is reported with effect size and CI but explicitly labelled exploratory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Feasibility tiers\\n\\nThis deliverable is **design-only**. No experiment is executed; no runtime or accuracy figures are reported. The orders below describe what execution *would* entail, not what it has produced.\\n\\n| Tier | Cells | Lengths | Tasks \\u00d7 seeds | Approx GPU-hour order | Compute binding |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **Tier-1 (minimum confident answer)** | Core 2\\u00d72 + L0a + L2b; primary contrast L3\\u00d7U_B vs L2b\\u00d7U_B | 16k only | 4 \\u00d7 3 | Low tens of GPU-hours on a single A100/H100; long-context forward pass dominates at 16k | Single forward pass per cell |\\n| **Tier-2** | Adds L4 retrieval-frequency sweep; adds length sweep | 4k / 16k / 64k / 128k (128k downscoped if VRAM-bound) | 4 \\u00d7 3 | Several \\u00d7 Tier-1 | Wall-clock \\u00d7 retrieval-frequency at 128k |\\n| **Tier-3** | Adds L2c; U_B unit-boundary sub-cell; U_G learned gate | All of the above | 4 \\u00d7 3 | Several \\u00d7 again, now U_G training dominates | U_G controlled training + 128k evaluation |\\n\\nWithin Tier-3, the U_B unit-boundary sub-cell and the U_G learned-gate extension are deliberately contained: gating adds parameters but the training data, seed, and epochs are fixed across cells that include it.\\n\\n**Offline / analytically tractable decomposition.** The U-axis (unit / pooling / similarity) and the L4 retrieval-frequency sweep are runnable on a fixed short context (4k\\u20138k) using only retrieval-quality metrics (precision@K, recall@K) and passkey \\u2014 no real long-context generation. Early signal at near-zero cost precedes the bulk Tier-1 evaluation.\\n\\nThis deliverable is design-only; no runtime measurements are recorded.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Threats to validity and limitations\\n\\n### 7.1 Assumptions (verbatim)\\n\\n- **A1** InfLLM = training-free KV-cache architecture with cached memory units + per-step dynamic top-k retrieval. Pending reconfirmation against the paper (U1).\\n- **A2** A single frozen open long-context LM (\\u2265128k native window). Researcher to confirm.\\n- **A3** Inference-time policy holds for L0 through L3; U_G preferred analytic; if learned, fixed corpus / seed / epochs.\\n- **A4** Memory = cached past K/V units indexed by hidden-state query; not parametric memory injection.\\n- **A5** Default task battery (passkey / NIAH / multi-hop QA / long summarization) is interchangeable if the researcher prefers alternatives.\\n- **A6** Attention-sink baseline L2b uses a small fixed number of initial-token sink KVs (StreamingLLM convention); sink count tuned once and frozen.\\n\\n### 7.2 Uncertainties (verbatim)\\n\\n- **U1** Reference fidelity of L3\\u00d7U_B to the published InfLLM specification; reduced severity now that L2b makes the contrast robust to spec drift on the dynamic-retrieval side.\\n- **U2** Base model identity affects absolute performance, not the comparative interpretation.\\n- **U3** 128k feasibility depends on VRAM; may move to Tier-3.\\n- **U4** Whether to include learned U_G gate.\\n- **U5** Statistical unit for paired bootstrap (items vs seeds).\\n- **U6** Whether retrieval-frequency (L4) is core or extension \\u2014 currently Tier-2.\\n- **U7** Sink count for L2b: default is tune-once + freeze; researcher may prefer a sink-count sweep in Tier-3.\\n\\n### 7.3 Additional threats to validity\\n\\n- **V1 \\u2014 Reference fidelity without source materials.** This deliverable was produced without the InfLLM paper or code in the workspace. The L3\\u00d7U_B reference cell is reconstructed from public description (see A1). Specific retention / eviction rules, top-k schedule, training-free claim, and how retrieval interacts with the base model's positional encoding cannot be reconfirmed in-repo. Mitigation in \\u00a78.\\n- **V2 \\u2014 Compressed headroom.** Modern long-context base models may already capture a substantial portion of the long-context behavior that memory mechanisms add. The dynamic-retrieval signal can therefore be small in absolute terms even when meaningful as a factor. The length sweep (4k / 16k / 64k / 128k) plus depth-bin decomposition lets us see whether the L3 \\u2212 L2b gap widens or narrows with length; this is the cleanest signal of whether F1 has any headroom to claim. Where headroom is small, the deliverable is the *comparative \\u0394 with bootstrap CIs and effect sizes*, not the absolute number.\\n- **V3 \\u2014 Confounding between similarity metric and pooling choice under U_B.** Under U_B, mean-pooled query + cosine similarity is the default; switching to last-token + dot-product conflates two factors at once. Mitigation: factor the similarity sub-cell out as its own row, and report U_T vs U_B as the primary granularity contrast, with the similarity sub-cell as a secondary decomposition. Effect-size reporting at the similarity sub-cell is exploratory to pre-registration and is not used to overturn the primary contrast.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Clarity note: A1 is load-bearing, the design mitigates A1\\n\\nA1 (faithful reconstruction of InfLLM from public description without the paper or code in the repo) is the central load-bearing assumption. If A1 were strongly wrong \\u2014 that is, if the published InfLLM spec uses a substantially different retrieval rule, retention schedule, or KV-cache layout than the reconstruction \\u2014 the L3\\u00d7U_B reference cell itself would be misspecified, and any direct claim about InfLLM would inherit that misspecification.\\n\\nThe design mitigates this in two complementary ways:\\n\\n1. **The primary contrast is direction-anchored, not absolute.** L3 \\u2212 L2b asks whether *dynamic retrieval* as a mechanism class outperforms the strongest realistic no-dynamic-retrieval competitor (attention-sink + sliding window). The direction of the contrast survives small spec drift: moderate misspecification of the L3 cell cannot flip a dynamic-retrieval effect into a no-dynamic-retrieval effect, because both cells are instantiated within the design and both reconstructions are anchored in public description. The contrast is robust to A1 misspecification as long as the L3 cell implements *some* dynamic query-conditioned retrieval on cached units \\u2014 the most stable part of the public description across reasonable reconstructions.\\n2. **Pre-registration locks the contrast and the metric set.** Before opening data, we register L3 \\u00d7 U_B \\u2212 L2b \\u00d7 U_B as primary. If post-hoc we discover a more faithful InfLLM reconstruction, it is reported separately as exploratory rather than allowed to redefine the primary contrast.\\n\\nCombined, these measures mean that *even if A1 is partially misspecified, the primary conclusion still answers the public question honestly*: it tells us how much dynamic retrieval contributes on top of the strongest realistic non-dynamic memory baseline, with the comparative rather than absolute interpretation. A1's severity is therefore reduced from \\\"blocks the experiment\\\" to \\\"shapes the absolute numbers and the precise L3 cell, but not the comparative direction.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n*End of design-only deliverable. No experiments were executed; no runtime, accuracy, or other empirical numbers are reported.*\", \"evidence\": \"Repository inspection (inspect_repository on /workspace plus recursive finds for *infllm*, *README*, *paper*, *track.md`) returned only /workspace/task.json (433 bytes), whose own documents.track.md field is the placeholder \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No InfLLM paper, code, configs, READMEs, notes, scripts, or datasets exist anywhere accessible; network is disabled, no GPU, 24 CPUs, Python 3.12.3 present, no project deps preinstalled. The design is constructed from public-description knowledge of InfLLM (training-free KV-cache memory + dynamic top-k retrieval), with that gap flagged as A1 / U1 and the primary contrast direction-anchored against attention-sink + sliding window (L2b) so that the comparison is robust to small reconstruction drift.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1 InfLLM = training-free KV-cache architecture with cached memory units + per-step dynamic top-k retrieval. Pending reconfirmation against the paper (U1).\", \"A2 A single frozen open long-context LM (>=128k native window). Researcher to confirm.\", \"A3 Inference-time policy holds for L0 through L3; U_G preferred analytic; if learned, fixed corpus / seed / epochs.\", \"A4 Memory = cached past K/V units indexed by hidden-state query; not parametric memory injection.\", \"A5 Default task battery (passkey / NIAH / multi-hop QA / long summarization) is interchangeable if the researcher prefers alternatives.\", \"A6 Attention-sink baseline L2b uses a small fixed number of initial-token sink KVs (StreamingLLM convention); sink count tuned once and frozen.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1 Reference fidelity of L3 x U_B to the published InfLLM specification; reduced severity now that L2b makes the contrast robust to spec drift on the dynamic-retrieval side.\", \"U2 Base model identity affects absolute performance, not the comparative interpretation.\", \"U3 128k feasibility depends on VRAM; may move to Tier-3.\", \"U4 Whether to include learned U_G gate.\", \"U5 Statistical unit for paired bootstrap (items vs seeds).\", \"U6 Whether retrieval-frequency (L4) is core or extension - currently Tier-2.\", \"U7 Sink count for L2b: default is tune-once + freeze; researcher may prefer a sink-count sweep in Tier-3.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-f54f8093-3eae-4584-af96-a7dba2983d8d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
